the%20next%20step&email=3D${email}&campaign=3Dtob_poll_1&v=3D1]=0A=0AOne cl=
ick is enough. I=E2=80=99ll share what we hear in a future issue.=0A=0ABill=
=0A=0A01The Shift=0A=0A=0AWhich Warning Could Delay Your Next Customer Orde=
r=3F=0A=0AMove from a list of alerts to a short, evidence-backed review of =
warnings that=0Acould affect customer commitments.=0A=0AAn operator may get=
 a machine warning, a vague supplier ETA, and a quality note=0Ain three dif=
ferent systems. Each matters in its own domain. But a planner needs=0Ato kn=
ow which open orders use that line, part, or lot, what inventory or=0Aalter=
nate capacity exists, and whether the warning is confirmed. A generic alert=
=0Acount can=E2=80=99t answer that.=0A=0ANot every signal needs AI. Determi=
nistic matching and current source records=0Aestablish the order, part, lot=
, date, quantity, and formal hold. The narrower=0Ashift is to make the cons=
equence and the missing fact visible to the right=0Aperson, so they can inv=
estigate before a customer promise changes.=0A=0ASarno=E2=80=99s distributo=
r example below puts a clock on the Shift: an order can be=0Arecorded yet r=
each fulfillment too late to change the delivery outcome. The=0AStack appli=
es that timing test to other warnings.=0A=0AWhich warnings actually touch a=
 promise=3F The answer is in the order records, not=0Athe alert count.=0A=
=0A02From the Floor=0A=0A=0ARecording the Order Isn=E2=80=99t the Same as A=
cting on It.=0A=0ADon Sarno of Sonepar on why a signal must reach fulfillme=
nt before its action=0Awindow closes.=0A=0ADon Sarno, Sonepar=E2=80=99s sen=
ior vice president of digital enterprise, describes an=0Aorder arriving at =
9 p.m. for delivery the next morning. In a Distribution=0AStrategy Group in=
terview=0A[https://distributionstrategy.com/2025/04/inside-sonepars-ai-stra=
tegy-a-conversation-with-don-sarno/],=0Ahe asks:=0A=0A=E2=80=9CDoes that ti=
cket just sit on a printer somewhere until someone comes in at 6:30=0Ain th=
e morning=3F=E2=80=9D=0A=0ADon Sarno=0A=0ASVP, Digital Enterprise, Sonepar.=
 Distribution Strategy Group, April 2025=0A[https://distributionstrategy.co=
m/2025/04/inside-sonepars-ai-strategy-a-conversation-with-don-sarno/]=0A=0A=
If it does, the delivery truck may already be on the road. An order can be=
=0Arecorded and still reach fulfillment too late for the team to act.=0A=0A=
The takeaway=0A=0AThe Stack shows how to check machine, supplier, and quali=
ty warnings against=0Apromised orders, then route unclear facts to the pers=
on who can verify them.=0ASarno=E2=80=99s case illustrates a distribution r=
isk; it is not a reported Jev result.=0A=0A03The Stack=0A=0A=0ATurn Warning=
s into a Short Review List=0A=0AMatch the records with software you already=
 have. Let Jev by TypeSafe label the=0Amessy notes for a person to review.=
=0A=0AA supplier says a part may arrive later next week. Which promised ord=
ers use=0Athat part, and who needs to check the date while there is still t=
ime to act=3F=0A=0AStart with one product family. Use your existing reporti=
ng or simple code to=0Aconnect open orders to machine, supplier, and qualit=
y notes by part, lot, or=0Awork-order number. Keep the unmatched notes for =
human review.=0A=0AWhat Jev is, in plain terms=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AJev by Type=
Safe is a focused AI model you can add to that workflow.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AY=
our software calls it through an API, which is simply a way to send a quest=
ion=0Aand get an answer back.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AA chatbot can write a summar=
y. Jev instead selects from choices you define, such=0Aas possible delay, c=
onfirmed delay, resolved, or unclear.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AIt can work alongsid=
e an AI assistant, but it doesn=E2=80=99t run inside one or require=0Aone.=
=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AThe useful part is making vague wording easier for a pers=
on to review.=0A=0AFor the supplier note, Jev could label the wording possi=
ble delay rather than=0Ainvent a confirmed delivery date. Put that label be=
side the original message,=0Athe affected order, the missing fact, and the =
person who can check it.=0APurchasing confirms the arrival date; planning d=
ecides whether the order needs=0Aattention.=0A=0AThe same approach can sort=
 maintenance and quality notes for review. Jev doesn=E2=80=99t=0Aconnect re=
cords, calculate stock, release a quality hold, or make the final=0Adecisio=
n. This is an example workflow, not a reported customer result.=0A=0AThe sa=
me check, three kinds of warning=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AMachine: A warning says a=
 line may be down for the next shift. Link the line to=0Aits work orders an=
d promised customer orders. Jev labels what the maintenance=0Anote actually=
 says; maintenance confirms recovery, and planning checks alternate=0Acapac=
ity before changing a promise. A review card might show the affected order,=
=0Amachine note, last update, open question, and maintenance/planning owner=
. This=0Ais a hypothetical card, not an observed customer result.=0A=0A=C2=
=A0=0A=0ASupplier: A message says a critical part may arrive =E2=80=9Clater=
 next week.=E2=80=9D Link the=0Apart to on-hand stock and upcoming builds. =
Treat that date as uncertain until=0Apurchasing confirms it. Jev can label =
the language; it can=E2=80=99t calculate inventory=0Acover without supplied=
 records.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AQuality: A lot tied to a near-term shipment is a=
waiting retest. Jev may classify=0Athe free-text note, but the formal QMS h=
old remains in force until the quality=0Aowner releases it.=0A=0AHow to try=
 it, in four steps=0A=0A1. Pick one product family. Pull sample records for=
 it and one small set of=0Acurrent exceptions. Keep the source IDs and time=
stamps.=0A=0A2. Connect warnings to orders. Use your existing reporting or =
simple code to=0Amatch warnings to actual orders. Calculate dates and quant=
ities outside Jev, and=0Atreat unmatched records as unknown, not safe.=0A=
=0A3. Try one narrow label. Run Jev on the matched notes for a single label=
. Show=0Athe label next to the original evidence, never in place of it.=0A=
=0A4. Compare before you act. Check the advisory list against your team=E2=
=80=99s normal=0Areview before you change priorities or customer commitment=
s.=0A=0AStart here with Jev by TypeSafe=0A[https://docs.typesafe.ai/introdu=
ction/quickstart]=0A=0ATypeSafe=E2=80=99s quickstart shows the Playground, =
your API key, and your first API=0Acall.=0A=0ABefore you use live data=0A=
=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AIf a developer on your team builds with a coding agent, Type=
Safe=E2=80=99s agent-skill=0Aguide [https://docs.typesafe.ai/agent-skill] h=
elps that agent use TypeSafe.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=0AInstalling the skill doesn=
=E2=80=99t create an API account or connect your ERP or QMS.=0A=0A=C2=A0=0A=
=0AFollow your company=E2=80=99s data rules before you send live order note=
s to an outside=0Aservice.=0A=0AThe hard rules live in the matching logic. =
The judgment lives in the label.=0A=0AThe Operations Brief=0A=0ABy Bill Mur=
